From 9beec8cbd6c5ef5868b1c7bbc3a35970aebee977 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 23:48:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DVDInputStreamBluray, VideoRenderers: the menus of an HDR
 playlist of an Ultra HD Blu-ray are drawn in PQ

The graphics of a disc are made for the video they go over. On an Ultra HD Blu-ray whose playlist
plays HDR10 or Dolby Vision video, its menus (the interactive graphics of HDMV) and what its BD-J
application draws are made the way its subtitles are: BT.2020 colours in the ST 2084 (PQ) signal,
laid over the video as they are. Kodi already takes the subtitles so (the PGS of a PQ video, drawn
straight onto the picture: m_isHDROverlay); the menus it took as sRGB. Over HDR they went through the
GUI's composite, sRGB to PQ at the GUI's white: a white made at 203 cd/m2 came out at about 60, the
whole menu grey, and its colours widened a second time; on an SDR display they came out dark. The
palette of the menus was converted with BT.601 coefficients, as for every disc.

The range of the disc's graphics (BlurayGraphicsRange.h) is the one of the video that the playlist's
STN table gives for the play item being played (dynamic_range_type: HDR10 and Dolby Vision are PQ),
and before a playlist is known, what a BD-J disc draws while it starts, the one that the disc
declares for its start (index.bdmv, initial_dynamic_range_type). It is kept while a playlist is
changed, and every change is told in kodi.log. In PQ, the palette of a menu is converted with the
coefficients of BT.2020 (as FFmpeg converts the subtitles of the same video), and the menu's
pictures, palette and ARGB, are HDR overlays: drawn as authored over HDR10, into the picture of
native Dolby Vision, and converted to sRGB for an SDR display with Kodi's own conversion of a PQ
palette (reference white at sRGB white, hue kept). For an ARGB picture, which had no HDR path, the
straight-alpha limited-range encode of a limited output is added, and the conversion to sRGB of a
whole picture takes that conversion from tables (ConvertPQPixelsToSRGB(): a picture of 1920x1080 in
9 to 60 ms instead of 33 to 200). A disc whose playlists are SDR, and every Blu-ray before Ultra HD
(whose fields are 0), are drawn as before, byte for byte.

Kodi's colour functions of OverlayRendererUtil.cpp (the PQ and sRGB curves, the conversion of a PQ
palette, the light of a bitmap subtitle) are moved, unchanged, into OverlayColour.h, so that a test
can build them alone.

Tests (outside the tree): menu_colour_test, with the flags of the host and those of the image
(-O3 -march=x86-64): the range of each dynamic_range_type; every Y'CbCr of an SDR palette byte for
byte as before, and of a PQ one as BT.2020 inverted from its definition (0 off of 16777216); the
moved functions byte for byte as before for every colour; ConvertPQPixelsToSRGB() as the
conversion of one colour for every one of the 16777216 colours. menu_frame, on llvmpipe and zink with
Kodi's composite shader of the tree: a menu made in PQ over a PQ picture, full and limited range,
is drawn as BT.2020 and ST 2084 give it within the precision of 8-bit graphics (3 codes of 10 bits;
the worst was 3) as an HDR overlay, and was off by up to 378 codes through the GUI (its white at 60
cd/m2 instead of 201, a highlight orange at 57 instead of 128); a fully transparent picture changes
no byte either way.

The fault was found by SamuriHL and pannal in their builds for CoreELEC (the menus of UHD discs
grey and washed, made in PQ and taken as sRGB), and the range of a playlist is where SamuriHL reads
it too.
